---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { SITE } from "../data/site";

const stack = [
  { name: ".NET 10", role: "Runtime" },
  { name: "Avalonia 12", role: "Cross platform UI" },
  { name: "SQLite", role: "Local storage" },
  { name: "Markdig", role: "Markdown" },
  { name: "LLama.cpp", role: "Local AI, WIP" },
  { name: "ONNX Runtime", role: "Embeddings, WIP" },
  { name: "CommunityToolkit.Mvvm", role: "MVVM" },
];
---

<Base
  title="Mnemo for developers, architecture and contributing"
  description="Mnemo is an open source Avalonia and .NET desktop app with a modular architecture. Learn how it is built and how to contribute."
>
  <section class="section">
    <div class="container-narrow">
      <span class="eyebrow">For developers</span>
      <h1>An open codebase you can actually navigate</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        Mnemo is a cross platform desktop app built with Avalonia 12 and .NET
        10, licensed under Apache 2.0. The architecture is deliberately boring
        in the good way: three layers, clear contracts, and features that live
        in self contained modules.
      </p>
      <div class="dev-cta">
        <a class="btn btn-primary" href={SITE.github} rel="noopener">Browse the source</a>
        <a class="btn btn-secondary" href={SITE.goodFirstIssues} rel="noopener">Good first issues</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section-soft">
    <div class="container">
      <h2>Three layers, one direction</h2>
      <p class="lede">
        Dependencies point inward. The core knows nothing about the outside
        world, infrastructure implements its contracts, and the UI consumes
        both.
      </p>
      <div class="arch">
        <div class="arch-layer">
          <h3>Mnemo.UI</h3>
          <p class="small muted">
            The Avalonia presentation layer. Views, view models, theming, and
            the module host.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="arch-arrow" aria-hidden="true">&darr; depends on</div>
        <div class="arch-layer">
          <h3>Mnemo.Infrastructure</h3>
          <p class="small muted">
            AI, storage, and knowledge services. The implementations behind the
            core's interfaces.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="arch-arrow" aria-hidden="true">&darr; depends on</div>
        <div class="arch-layer arch-core">
          <h3>Mnemo.Core</h3>
          <p class="small muted">
            Shared models, interfaces, and contracts. No external dependencies.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container split">
      <div>
        <h2>Features are modules</h2>
        <p class="muted">
          Notes, flashcards, mind maps: each feature is a self contained module
          under <code>/Modules</code>, discovered automatically through the
          <code>IModule</code> interface. A module brings its own views, view
          models, and services, and the host wires it up.
        </p>
        <p class="muted">
          This is also the extensibility story. The same mechanism that keeps
          first party features decoupled is what the planned Explore store
          builds on for third party extensions and themes.
        </p>
        <p class="muted">
          Across the codebase the patterns are consistent: MVVM, dependency
          injection, and async first APIs. The
          <a href={SITE.codingStandard} rel="noopener">coding standard</a>
          spells out the details.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h2 class="stack-heading">The stack</h2>
        <ul class="stack-list">
          {
            stack.map((item) => (
              <li>
                <code>{item.name}</code>
                <span class="small muted">{item.role}</span>
              </li>
            ))
          }
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section-soft">
    <div class="container-narrow">
      <h2>Contributing</h2>
      <p class="muted">
        Contributions are welcome, and not only code. Bug reports from real
        study sessions are some of the most useful contributions there are,
        especially on Linux and macOS where builds get less testing.
      </p>
      <ul class="checklist">
        <li>
          Found a bug? <a href={SITE.issues} rel="noopener">Open an issue</a>
          with steps to reproduce
        </li>
        <li>
          Want to write code? Start with the
          <a href={SITE.goodFirstIssues} rel="noopener">good first issues</a>
        </li>
        <li>
          Read the <a href={SITE.codingStandard} rel="noopener">coding standard</a>
          before opening a pull request
        </li>
        <li>
          The <a href={SITE.docs} rel="noopener">documentation</a> covers both
          users and contributors
        </li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>

<style>
  .dev-cta {
    display: flex;
    gap: 0.75rem;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    margin-top: 1.75rem;
  }

  .arch {
    max-width: 560px;
    display: grid;
    gap: 0.6rem;
    margin-top: 2rem;
  }

  .arch-layer {
    background: var(--bg-raised);
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
    border-radius: var(--radius-md);
    padding: 1.1rem 1.4rem;
    box-shadow: var(--shadow-sm);
  }

  .arch-layer h3 {
    font-family: ui-monospace, "Cascadia Code", Menlo, Consolas, monospace;
    font-size: 0.95rem;
    margin-bottom: 0.25rem;
  }

  .arch-layer p {
    margin: 0;
  }

  .arch-core {
    border-color: var(--accent-soft-border);
    background: var(--accent-soft);
  }

  .arch-arrow {
    text-align: center;
    font-size: 0.8rem;
    color: var(--ink-faint);
  }

  .split {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 1.3fr 1fr;
    gap: 4rem;
    align-items: start;
  }

  .stack-list {
    list-style: none;
    margin: 0;
    padding: 0;
    display: grid;
    gap: 0.6rem;
  }

  .stack-list li {
    display: flex;
    align-items: baseline;
    justify-content: space-between;
    gap: 1rem;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border);
    padding-bottom: 0.6rem;
  }

  @media (max-width: 920px) {
    .split {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
      gap: 2.5rem;
    }
  }
</style>
